The Code
def unique_paths(m, n):
    dp = [[1] * n for _ in range(m)]   # fill with 1s (base cases handled)

    for i in range(1, m):
        for j in range(1, n):
            dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]

    return dp[m-1][n-1]

print(unique_paths(3, 7))   # → 28
print(unique_paths(3, 2))   # → 3
print(unique_paths(1, 1))   # → 1
Time: O(m × n) — Space: O(m × n)

Space can be reduced to O(n) with a rolling array — we only ever look at the current and previous row.
def unique_paths_opt(m, n):
    row = [1] * n
    for _ in range(1, m):
        for j in range(1, n):
            row[j] += row[j-1]    # row[j] = old row[j] + new row[j-1]
    return row[-1]

Adjusting the Recurrence
Only change: if a cell has an obstacle, it contributes 0 paths.
def unique_paths_obstacles(grid):
    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])
    if grid[0][0] == 1 or grid[m-1][n-1] == 1:
        return 0    # start or end is blocked

    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(m)]
    dp[0][0] = 1

    # First column: if any obstacle, all cells below it are blocked
    for i in range(1, m):
        dp[i][0] = dp[i-1][0] if grid[i][0] == 0 else 0

    # First row: same logic
    for j in range(1, n):
        dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-1] if grid[0][j] == 0 else 0

    # Fill the rest
    for i in range(1, m):
        for j in range(1, n):
            if grid[i][j] == 1:
                dp[i][j] = 0         # obstacle: no paths through here
            else:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i][j-1]

    return dp[m-1][n-1]

The Backtracking Template
def explore(partial_solution):
    if is_complete(partial_solution):
        record(partial_solution)          # found a solution!
        return

    for choice in get_choices(partial_solution):
        if is_valid(partial_solution, choice):
            partial_solution.add(choice)    # make the choice
            explore(partial_solution)       # explore further
            partial_solution.remove(choice) # undo (backtrack)

The “undo” step is what makes backtracking work. We explore, retreat, and try something else.

Maze — Python Code
def solve(r, c, path):
  if not (0 &lt;= r &lt; 16 and 0 &lt;= c &lt; 16) 
    or maze[r][c] == 'X' or (r, c) in path:
   return False

  path.append((r, c))

  # Base Case: Found the Exit
  if maze[r][c] == 'E':
   return True

  # Explore: Up, Down, Left, Right
  for dr, dc in [(0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1), (-1, 0)]:
   if solve(r + dr, c + dc, path):
    return True

  # Backtrack
  path.pop()
  return False

solution_path = []
rc = solve(0, 0, solution_path)
if rc: print("Path to exit:", solution_path)
else: print("No path found.")

Sudoku — Simplified Code
def solve_sudoku(board):
    empty = find_empty(board)
    if not empty:
        return True   # all cells filled → solution!

    row, col = empty

    for digit in range(1, 10):
        if is_valid_placement(board, row, col, digit):
            board[row][col] = digit

            # PRUNING: check if any cell now has 0 options
            if not any_cell_has_no_options(board):
                if solve_sudoku(board):   # recurse
                    return True

            board[row][col] = 0   # ← backtrack

    return False

any_cell_has_no_options is the pruning check — it looks ahead without committing.

Graph Colouring — Code
def is_safe(v, graph, c):
    for neighbor in graph[v]:
        if colors[neighbor] == c:
            return False
    return True


def solve_graph_coloring(graph, k, v=0):
    # Base Case: All vertices are colored
    if v == len(graph):
        return True

    # Try different colors for vertex v
    for c in range(1, k + 1):
        if is_safe(v, graph, c):
            colors[v] = c  # Choose

            if solve_graph_coloring(graph, k, v + 1):  # Explore
                return True

            colors[v] = 0  # Backtrack (Un-choose)

    return False

graph = [[2], [3, 4], [0, 5], [1, 4], [1, 3], [2]]
colors = [0] * len(graph)
print(solve_graph_coloring(graph, 2), colors)
colors = [0] * len(graph)
print(solve_graph_coloring(graph, 3), colors)

🟩 SECTION 4: DATA & ADVANCED
Table: Automatic Data Mapping
What makes this Table special?
In ezui, the Table is “Smart.” You don’t have to tell it what the headers are or manually type every row. Instead, you give it a List of Objects, and the Table handles the rest.
Step 1: The ITable Contract
To show your data in a table, your class (like Student or Book) must “implement” the ITable interface. This tells the table how to “read” your object.
public class Student implements ITable {
    private String id, name, grade;

    // The Table calls this to build the top header row
    public String[] getHeaders() { 
        return new String[]{"ID", "Student Name", "Current Grade"}; 
    }

    // The Table calls this to fill in each row
    public String[] getValues() { 
        return new String[]{id, name, grade}; 
    }
}

Code Anatomy - Handling Multiple Errors
try {
    String input = scanner.nextLine();
    int num = Integer.parseInt(input); // Potential NumberFormatException
    System.out.println(10 / num);      // Potential ArithmeticException
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: Please enter a valid integer.");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: Cannot divide by zero.");
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Something else went wrong: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    System.out.println("Cleanup code can go here.");
}

Pro-Tip: Always catch specific exceptions first. If you catch the general Exception class first, the more specific ones below it will never be reached.

Defining and Throwing Your Own Exceptions

You don’t have to wait for the JVM to find an error. You can trigger one yourself to enforce rules.
The throw Keyword:

public void setAge(int age) {
    if (age &lt; 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative!");
    }
    this.age = age;
}

This is “Defensive Programming”—stopping bad data before it ruins your program’s state.

Exam Prep - “What is the Output?”
Question: What prints if the user enters “0”?
try {
    int x = 5 / Integer.parseInt(input);
    System.out.println("A");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("B");
} finally {
    System.out.println("C");
}

Answer: B then C. “A” is skipped because the error happens on the first line of the try.

Pattern: One Thread, One Output Element
@cuda.jit
def scale(arr, factor):
    i = cuda.grid(1)
    if i &lt; arr.shape[0]:
        arr[i] = arr[i] * factor

The pattern every kernel this semester has used so far: launch (at least) as many threads as there are outputs, each thread computes exactly one
Simple to reason about, maps directly onto the hardware’s SIMT model (Sep 30), and is the right choice whenever the number of outputs is known ahead of time and reasonably launch-able (millions of threads is fine; billions can hit practical limits)
This is the default pattern — reach for it first, and only reach for something else when it doesn’t fit


~2 min. Deliberately named and framed as “the pattern” — students have been using it since Lab 4 without necessarily thinking of it as a reusable, nameable design choice. Naming it is the point of this slide.

From 1D to 2D: Box Blur
@cuda.jit
def box_blur_2d(img, out, H, W, radius):
    row, col = cuda.grid(2)
    if row &lt; H and col &lt; W:
        total = 0.0
        for dr in range(-radius, radius + 1):
            for dc in range(-radius, radius + 1):
                r = min(max(row + dr, 0), H - 1)   # clamp row
                c = min(max(col + dc, 0), W - 1)   # clamp col
                total += img[r, c]
        out[row, col] = total / ((2 * radius + 1) ** 2)

Same idea as the 1D case, doubled: a (2·radius+1) × (2·radius+1) square neighborhood, clamped independently on both row and column
This is image convolution — the motivating example for this whole session. A box blur is the simplest possible convolution: every neighbor weighted equally
Radius 1 → 3×3 window (9 taps). Radius 2 → 5×5 window (25 taps). Bigger radius means more blur, but also a lot more memory reads per output pixel


~3 min. This is Lab 07’s Exercise 1 pattern, shown as a worked example. The nested loop (row neighborhood × col neighborhood) is the main new syntactic idea versus the 1D case — walk through it explicitly.

Indentation Is the Syntax


Java
if (score &gt;= 60) {
    System.out.println("Pass");
} else {
    System.out.println("Fail");
}

Python
if score &gt;= 60:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Fail")


No { } braces, no ; semicolons — a colon : opens a block, and indentation (consistent spaces) marks what’s inside it
Mixing tabs and spaces, or indenting inconsistently, is a real error in Python — it isn’t just a style preference like in Java
Colab auto-indents for you after a colon — trust it, don’t fight it


~3 min. This trips up Java developers hard in Week 1 — an accidentally-misaligned line is a silent bug, not a compiler error shouting at you. Emphasize it strongly.

For Loops, Side by Side


Java
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

Python
for i in range(5):
    print(i)


Java’s for-loop spells out three separate things: start, condition, increment
Python’s range(5) bundles all three into one call: produces 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — five values, not including 5
range(start, stop) and range(start, stop, step) both work, same off-by-one convention: stop is never included


~3 min. The “stop is excluded” rule is the single most common off-by-one bug source this semester — call it out explicitly and have students predict range(2, 8)’s output before revealing it.

Looping Over Data Directly


Java
int[] scores = {88, 92, 75};
for (int s : scores) {
    System.out.println(s);
}

Python
scores = [88, 92, 75]
for s in scores:
    print(s)


Java’s enhanced for-loop and Python’s for x in list do the same thing: hand you each element in turn, no index needed
Need the index too? for i, s in enumerate(scores): gives you both — closest Python equivalent to a classic indexed loop


~2 min. Students will reach for for i in range(len(scores)): print(scores[i]) out of habit — show it works, then show for s in scores: is the idiomatic version and gets used almost everywhere in this course.

While Loops and Conditionals


Java
int n = 10;
while (n &gt; 0) {
    n--;
}
if (n == 0 && ready) {
    n = 1;
}

Python
n = 10
while n &gt; 0:
    n -= 1

if n == 0 and ready:
    n = 1



&&, ||, ! in Java become the words and, or, not in Python
No n-- or n++ shorthand in Python — write n -= 1 / n += 1
== still means “equals” in both languages — that one didn’t change


~2 min. Quick slide, mostly a reference table students will return to — don’t over-explain, just point out the word-vs-symbol swap.

Functions, Side by Side


Java
static double average(
        int a, int b) {
    return (a + b) / 2.0;
}

Python
def average(a, b):
    return (a + b) / 2


def replaces the return-type-plus-name pattern — Python functions don’t declare a return type
No static, no access modifiers (public/private) — Python’s simplicity here is deliberate, not something being hidden
Python allows default arguments: def average(a, b=0): lets a caller omit b entirely — Java requires overloading a whole separate method for that


~3 min. Default arguments are new territory for a Java-only background — worth a concrete second example (greet(name, greeting="Hello"), called both with and without greeting).

Your First Flask Route
from flask import Flask, jsonify
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/hello")
def hello():
    return jsonify({"message": "Hello, World!"})

@app.route("/users/&lt;int:user_id&gt;")
def get_user(user_id):
    return jsonify({"id": user_id, "name": "Alice"})

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True, port=5000)

@app.route("/hello") is a decorator — it wraps the function written right below it. Read it as: “whenever an HTTP request arrives at /hello, call this function and send back whatever it returns.”
&lt;int:user_id&gt; in the URL captures part of the path as an argument — a request to /users/42 calls get_user(42).
jsonify(...) takes a Python dict and turns it into a proper JSON HTTP response.
Run with python app.py, then visit http://localhost:5000/hello in a browser.


Timing: 5 min. The decorator explanation is genuinely new depth for this audience — don’t assume “decorator” is a familiar Python concept, spell it out plainly as “wraps the function below it,” since it’s one of the more abstract syntax ideas in the whole course.

Connecting Flask to MongoDB
from flask import Flask, jsonify
from pymongo import MongoClient

app    = Flask(__name__)
client = MongoClient("mongodb+srv://...")
db     = client["mydb"]

@app.route("/employees")
def get_employees():
    docs = list(db["employees"].find({}, {"_id": 0}))
    return jsonify(docs)

@app.route("/employees/&lt;dept&gt;")
def get_by_dept(dept):
    docs = list(db["employees"].find({"dept": dept}, {"_id": 0}))
    return jsonify(docs)

Same MongoClient and .find() syntax from Module 5’s Lab 9 — nothing new there.
list(...) around .find() is necessary because jsonify() needs an actual list, not MongoDB’s lazy cursor object.
This is exactly the pattern your capstone’s flask_app.py uses for its five JSON API endpoints.


Timing: 4 min. Say plainly: “this slide is the shape of five of your capstone’s endpoints, just with different collection names and filters.”

Practice 1: Spot the Race Condition
class Counter {
    private int total = 0;

    public void add(int amount) {
        total = total + amount;   // is this line safe?
    }

    public int getTotal() {
        return total;
    }
}
Two threads both call add(5) on the same Counter object, 1,000 times each, at the same time.
With a partner (2–3 min): is add() safe to call from multiple threads at once? If not, name the exact three-step breakdown (Session 3) that causes the problem here.

Timing: 4 min including pair discussion. Don’t reveal the answer yet — let pairs actually argue about it first.

Practice 2: Trace the Interleaving
static int balance = 100;

// Thread A: withdraw(30)     // Thread B: withdraw(50)
int b = balance;               int b = balance;
b = b - 30;                    b = b - 50;
balance = b;                   balance = b;
Both threads run withdraw on the same balance, unsynchronized, starting at balance = 100.
With a partner (2–3 min): find an interleaving of these six lines that leaves balance at an incorrect value. Write out the timeline, the way Session 3 did for the counter example.

Timing: 4 min including pair discussion. If pairs finish early, ask them to also find an interleaving that happens to produce the correct result — reinforcing Session 3’s “same code, different outcomes” point.

Practice 3: Will This Fix Work?
A student “fixes” the Counter from Practice 1 like this:
class Counter {
    private int total = 0;

    public void add(int amount) {
        synchronized (new Object()) {   // ← added this
            total = total + amount;
        }
    }
}
With a partner (2–3 min): does wrapping the line in synchronized (new Object()) actually fix the race condition? Why or why not?

Timing: 4 min including pair discussion. This is the trickiest of the three problems — expect some pairs to say “yes, it’s synchronized now” without noticing the new keyword. That’s exactly the misconception this problem is designed to surface.

Method-Level vs. Block-Level Locking
synchronized can mark a whole method, or wrap just part of one:
// Method-level: the entire method body is protected
static synchronized void increment() {
    counter++;
}

// Block-level: only the marked block is protected, on a chosen lock object
static void increment() {
    synchronized (lockObject) {
        counter++;
    }
}

Method-level is shorthand for “lock on this (or the class, for static methods), for the whole method body.”
Block-level lets you (a) lock on a specific object rather than always this, and (b) protect only the few lines that actually touch shared data, leaving the rest of the method free to run unsynchronized.
Block-level is usually the better default once your methods do more than one thing — locking is a cost (next slide), and you only want to pay it for the part that’s actually shared.


Timing: 5 min. This slide is new depth beyond what was previously taught — students need to see block-level syntax since it’s the more common real-world pattern, not just the toy method-level example.

Deadlock: The Circular Wait
Locking introduces a new failure mode that didn’t exist before: two threads can each be waiting for a lock the other one holds, forever.
// Thread A                      // Thread B
synchronized (lockA) {           synchronized (lockB) {
    synchronized (lockB) { }         synchronized (lockA) { }  // ← DEADLOCK
}

Thread A grabs lockA, then tries to grab lockB — but Thread B already holds lockB and is trying to grab lockA.
Neither thread will ever release the lock it holds, because both are stuck waiting for the other lock first. Both threads freeze permanently.


Timing: 4 min. Walk the code example line by line — trace the circular wait out loud (or on the board): A holds lockA wants lockB, B holds lockB wants lockA. The picture and the prevention rule are the next slide.

Threads in Java — Option 1: Extend Thread
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Hello from " + getName());
    }
}

Subclass Thread and override run() with the work you want done.
Start it with new MyThread().start().
Works, but it ties your class to Thread — your class can no longer extend anything else, and “the work” and “how it’s run” are welded together.


Timing: ~2 min. Read the code aloud line by line — don’t assume the syntax is self-explanatory.

Threads in Java — Option 2: Implement Runnable (preferred)
Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Hello from lambda");
});
t.start();
t.join(); // wait for t to finish

Pass the work to new Thread(...) instead of subclassing. Your code stays independent of how it’s run.
() -&gt; { ... } is a lambda — shorthand for “here is a block of code, treat it as a value.” Read it as: “a nameless block of code that, when run, prints the message.”
Handing that block to new Thread(...) says “run this on a new thread.”


Timing: ~2 min.

Threads in Java — The Four Methods
Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Hello from lambda");
});
t.start();
t.join(); // wait for t to finish

new Thread(...) — creates the thread object. Nothing is running yet.
start() — actually launches the new thread. This is the only line that creates concurrency.
join() — makes the calling thread wait until this one finishes, before continuing.
run() — the code the thread executes. Never call run() directly — the next slide shows exactly why.


Timing: ~2 min. The lambda gloss on the previous slide carries the explanation; still worth restating verbally in your own words.

Tracing It: What Actually Happens
Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Hello from lambda");
});
t.start();
t.join();
System.out.println("Main thread continues");
Five steps. What does each thread do at each one? (next slide)

Timing: ~1 min. Read the code, then have students predict the order of the two printed lines before you show the table.

The Thread Lifecycle — How a Thread Moves Through It

Every thread you create moves through these states.

new Thread(...) puts it in New;
start() moves it to Runnable;
it becomes Running only when it actually gets to execute;
and it can bounce between Running and Blocked/Waiting many times before reaching Terminated.


Timing: ~2 min. Trace the diagram left to right against the table on the previous slide, state by state.

Step 1 — one particle


    Processing

      ▶ Run
      ⏸ Pause



    class Particle {
  float x, y, vy, r;
  color c;

  Particle(float startX) {
    x = startX;
    y = height + random(50);        // start below the bottom edge
    vy = -random(1, 3);             // rises upward
    r = random(6, 16);
    c = color(random(150, 255), random(100, 200), 255);
  }

  void move() {
    y += vy;
  }

  void display() {
    fill(c);
    noStroke();
    ellipse(x, y, r, r);
  }
}

Particle p1;

void setup() {
  size(400, 300);
  p1 = new Particle(200);
}

void draw() {
  background(15, 15, 35);
  p1.move();
  p1.display();
}





    Errors



One Particle, drifting upward and eventually off the top of the canvas. Its constructor picks random values for y, vy, r, and c, so every particle you build will look and move slightly differently.

Step 2 — recycle it when it leaves the screen


    Processing

      ▶ Run
      ⏸ Pause



    class Particle {
  float x, y, vy, r;
  color c;

  Particle(float startX) {
    x = startX;
    y = height + random(50);
    vy = -random(1, 3);
    r = random(6, 16);
    c = color(random(150, 255), random(100, 200), 255);
  }

  void move() {
    y += vy;
    if (y &lt; -r) {                   // recycle once it drifts off the top
      y = height + random(50);
      x = random(width);
    }
  }

  void display() {
    fill(c);
    noStroke();
    ellipse(x, y, r, r);
  }
}

Particle p1;

void setup() {
  size(400, 300);
  p1 = new Particle(200);
}

void draw() {
  background(15, 15, 35);
  p1.move();
  p1.display();
}





    Errors



Once y &lt; -r (the particle has drifted off the top), move() resets it to a new random position below the bottom edge, as if it were brand new. That’s what will let the animation run forever with a fixed number of particles, instead of them all eventually disappearing.

Step 3 — an array of forty particles


    Processing

      ▶ Run
      ⏸ Pause



    class Particle {
  float x, y, vy, r;
  color c;

  Particle(float startX) {
    x = startX;
    y = height + random(50);
    vy = -random(1, 3);
    r = random(6, 16);
    c = color(random(150, 255), random(100, 200), 255);
  }

  void move() {
    y += vy;
    if (y &lt; -r) {
      y = height + random(50);
      x = random(width);
    }
  }

  void display() {
    fill(c);
    noStroke();
    ellipse(x, y, r, r);
  }
}

Particle[] particles = new Particle[40];

void setup() {
  size(400, 300);
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; particles.length; i++) {
    particles[i] = new Particle(random(width));
  }
}

void draw() {
  background(15, 15, 35);
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; particles.length; i++) {
    particles[i].move();
    particles[i].display();
  }
}





    Errors



Particle[] particles is an array where every slot holds one whole Particle object, each with its own x, y, vy, r, and c. Read draw() first — it’s short on purpose: one loop, and inside it, two method calls, particles[i].move() then particles[i].display(). Everything interesting is hidden inside the Particle class — draw() doesn’t need to know how a particle moves or what color it is, only that every particle can move() and display() itself.
This is the same array-of-objects loop pattern as Blob[] blobs from last time, just with a class that has more fields, and enough objects (40, not 5) that it stops looking like a demo and starts looking like a piece.

Step 1 — one circle, loose variables


