}





    Errors



Three variables, x1/y1/r1, that only make sense together — nothing in the code says so except the shared 1 in their names.

Step 2 — three circles (the problem)


    Processing

      ▶ Run
      ⏸ Pause



    float x1 = 80,  y1 = 150, r1 = 40;
float x2 = 200, y2 = 100, r2 = 60;
float x3 = 320, y3 = 180, r3 = 25;

void setup() {
  size(400, 250);
}

void draw() {
  background(240);
  fill(90, 150, 220);
  noStroke();
  ellipse(x1, y1, r1, r1);
  ellipse(x2, y2, r2, r2);
  ellipse(x3, y3, r3, r3);
}





    Errors



Nine variables now, tied together only by naming convention. As the number of circles grows, so does the chance of a typo — using r2 where you meant r1. This is the signal worth learning to watch for: several variables that only make sense together, repeated once per “thing.” It means “this should be a class.”

Step 3 — write the class
class Blob {
  float x, y, r;

  Blob(float xPos, float yPos, float radius) {
    x = xPos;
    y = yPos;
    r = radius;
  }
}
Every Blob will have three float fields (x, y, r) and a constructor that takes three arguments to set them. This slide is just the blueprint — not runnable on its own yet, since nothing calls new Blob(...).

Step 4 — build one object


    Processing

      ▶ Run
      ⏸ Pause



    class Blob {
  float x, y, r;

  Blob(float xPos, float yPos, float radius) {
    x = xPos;
    y = yPos;
    r = radius;
  }
}

Blob b1;

void setup() {
  size(400, 250);
  b1 = new Blob(80, 150, 40);
}

void draw() {
  background(240);
  fill(90, 150, 220);
  noStroke();
  ellipse(b1.x, b1.y, b1.r, b1.r);
}





    Errors



