Identify the bug:
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= arr.length; i++) {
    IO.println(arr[i]);
}
What exception will this throw? Fix it.

Accumulator: Sum and Product
Sum of user inputs:
int sum = 0;

for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
    String input = IO.readln("Enter number " + i + ": ");
    int n = Integer.parseInt(input);
    sum = sum + n;
}

IO.println("Total: " + sum);
Product (factorial):
int product = 1;            // identity for multiplication

for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
    product = product * i;
}

IO.println("5! = " + product);   // 5! = 120

Walk through the sum example with a concrete run: if the user enters 3, 7, 1, 4, 2, the running sum is 0→3→10→11→15→17. Then contrast with product — same structure, different identity and operator. Ask students what would happen if product started at 0.

Accumulator: Maximum
Finding the largest value requires a slightly different identity:
String first = IO.readln("Enter number 1: ");
int max = Integer.parseInt(first);    // start with the first value

for (int i = 2; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
    String input = IO.readln("Enter number " + i + ": ");
    int n = Integer.parseInt(input);
    if (n &gt; max) {
        max = n;        // update only when a new champion is found
    }
}

IO.println("Maximum: " + max);






Note


For maximum, there is no numeric identity — starting at 0 would break if all inputs are negative. Start with the first element instead.





The “running champion” mental model: only the current best holds the trophy; it gets handed off whenever a bigger contender shows up. Starting at 0 is wrong if inputs can be negative (-5 &gt; 0 is false, so -5 would never become max). Starting with the first element is always safe.

The Counter Pattern
Template:
int count = 0;             // always starts at 0

for (...) {
    if (someCondition) {
        count = count + 1; // increment only when condition is met
    }
}

// use count AFTER the loop
Example: Count how many numbers from 1 to 100 are divisible by 7:
int count = 0;

for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 100; i++) {
    if (i % 7 == 0) {
        count++;
    }
}

IO.println("Divisible by 7: " + count);   // 14

The counter is an accumulator where the operation is always +1 and it’s conditional. Point out that count always starts at 0 — unlike max which starts at the first element. Also a good moment to reinforce i % 7 == 0 as the divisibility test (remainder is 0 → divides evenly).

The Sentinel Pattern
Template:
// Read once BEFORE the loop (prime the pump)
String input = IO.readln("...");

while (!input.equals(SENTINEL)) {
    // process input
    input = IO.readln("...");   // read again at the BOTTOM
}
Example: Read numbers until the user types "done", print each:
String line = IO.readln("Enter a number (or 'done'): ");

while (!line.equals("done")) {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(line);
    IO.println("You entered: " + n);
    line = IO.readln("Enter a number (or 'done'): ");
}

IO.println("Finished.");

Repeat the “prime the pump” explanation from deck 11. The sentinel pattern almost always uses while (not for) because you don’t know in advance how many iterations there will be. The two reads are easy to forget — drill the pattern: read → check → use → read → check → …

Sentinel: Input Validation Loop
A sentinel loop can also enforce valid input:
String input = IO.readln("Enter a positive number: ");
int n = Integer.parseInt(input);

while (n &lt;= 0) {
    IO.println("That's not positive. Try again.");
    input = IO.readln("Enter a positive number: ");
    n = Integer.parseInt(input);
}

IO.println("Got it: " + n);
The “sentinel” here is not a special value — it’s an invalid condition.

This is a very common real-world use case: keep asking until the answer is acceptable. Note that the structure is identical to the sentinel pattern — read before the loop, check, process (in this case, complain), read again at the bottom.

Combining Patterns: Average
Goal: read numbers from the user until "done", then print the average.
int sum = 0;      // accumulator
int count = 0;    // counter

String line = IO.readln("Enter a number (or 'done'): ");

while (!line.equals("done")) {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(line);
    sum = sum + n;    // accumulate
    count++;          // count
    line = IO.readln("Enter a number (or 'done'): ");
}

if (count &gt; 0) {
    IO.println("Average: " + (sum / count));
} else {
    IO.println("No numbers entered.");
}

Average = sum / count. You need BOTH an accumulator (for the total) and a counter (for how many). They run in the same loop body — the loop visits each value once, and both patterns update in the same pass. The guard (count &gt; 0) prevents division by zero if the user types “done” immediately.

Multi-Goal Loop
A single loop can track sum, count, and maximum all at once:
int sum = 0;
int count = 0;
int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;   // smallest possible int

String line = IO.readln("Enter a number (or 'done'): ");

while (!line.equals("done")) {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(line);
    sum = sum + n;
    count++;
    if (n &gt; max) { max = n; }
    line = IO.readln("Enter a number (or 'done'): ");
}

IO.println("Sum: " + sum + ", Count: " + count + ", Max: " + max);






Note


Integer.MIN_VALUE is the smallest int Java can represent (~−2 billion). It is always safe as the starting max because any real input will be larger.




Integer.MIN_VALUE solves the “what if all inputs are negative?” problem cleanly. Walk through a concrete example: inputs -3, -7, -1. If max started at 0, the answer would be 0 (wrong). With MIN_VALUE, it correctly finds -1.

Code Example — Grade Statistics
void printStats(int numStudents) {
    int sum = 0;
    int count = 0;
    int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= numStudents; i++) {
        String input = IO.readln("Enter grade for student " + i + ": ");
        int grade = Integer.parseInt(input);
        sum = sum + grade;
        count++;
        if (grade &gt; max) { max = grade; }
    }

    IO.println("Average: " + (sum / count));
    IO.println("Highest: " + max);
    IO.println("Total students: " + count);
}

void main() {
    printStats(5);
}

This example puts everything together: a method with a parameter (deck 09), a for loop (deck 12), and three loop patterns running simultaneously (accumulator for sum, counter for count, max tracker). Walk through it line by line and have students identify which part of the loop body belongs to which pattern.

The Problem with Repetition
Without loops, printing a countdown looks like this:
IO.println(5);
IO.println(4);
IO.println(3);
IO.println(2);
IO.println(1);
IO.println("Blast off!");
What if you needed to count down from 1 000?

Open with the visceral horror of copying code 1000 times. This is the gut-level motivation. Let students feel the pain before offering the relief. Don’t move on until someone laughs or groans.

The while Loop
while (condition) {
    // body — runs over and over as long as condition is true
}

Java checks the condition before every iteration
If true → run the body, then check again
If false → skip the body entirely and move on



Walk through the flowchart slowly. The condition is a gate that the program hits every single time before running the body — including the very first time. This is the core mental model. “Before every iteration” is worth saying three times.

The Countdown (Revisited)
int count = 5;
while (count &gt;= 1) {
    IO.println(count);
    count = count - 1;
}
IO.println("Blast off! 🚀");
Output:
5
4
3
2
1
Blast off! 🚀
Change count = 5 to count = 1000 and it still works — no copying!

Return to the opening example and show the payoff. The loop is counting DOWN, so the update is subtraction and the condition is &gt;=. Ask students: “What would happen if the update were count = count + 1 instead?” They should say: infinite loop.

The Counter Pattern
Goal: count how many words the user enters (stop at "done").
int count = 0;

String word = IO.readln("Enter a word (or 'done' to stop): ");

while (!word.equals("done")) {
    count = count + 1;
    word = IO.readln("Enter a word (or 'done' to stop): ");
}

IO.println("You entered " + count + " word(s).");

The counter is a special accumulator: instead of adding the value, we always add 1. Highlight that count starts at 0 before the loop. Also point out the structure: this loop is ALSO an example of the sentinel pattern — “done” is the stop signal. The next slide formalizes that.

The Sentinel Loop Pattern
A sentinel value is a special input that means “stop — we’re done”:
String word = IO.readln("Enter a word (or 'quit' to stop): ");  // ← prime the pump

while (!word.equals("quit")) {
    IO.println("You entered: " + word);
    word = IO.readln("Enter a word (or 'quit' to stop): ");     // ← read again at bottom
}

IO.println("Goodbye!");
The read happens twice — once before the loop, once at the bottom of the body.


The sentinel pattern trips students up because the read appears twice. Explain why: you need a value before the condition is checked for the first time (prime the pump), and you need a fresh value at the end of each iteration or the loop will run forever on the same word. If they forget the second read, it’s an infinite loop.

Infinite Loops
Forgetting the update:
int i = 1;
while (i &lt;= 5) {
    IO.println(i);
    // i never changes — condition stays true forever
}
Updating in the wrong direction:
int i = 1;
while (i &gt;= 1) {
    IO.println(i);
    i = i + 1;    // i grows → condition i &gt;= 1 is always true
}






Tip


Program seems frozen? You’re probably in an infinite loop. Press Ctrl + C to stop it.




Show both flavors: forgetting the update entirely, and updating in the wrong direction. The Ctrl+C tip is immediately practical and reassures students they aren’t breaking anything. Ask: “How would you fix each of these?”

Off-by-One Errors
Should this print 1 through 5, or 1 through 4?
int i = 1;
while (i &lt; 5) {      // &lt; vs &lt;=  ← easy to mix up!
    IO.println(i);
    i = i + 1;
}
Output: 1  2  3  4 — stops one short!
Fix: change &lt; to &lt;=:
while (i &lt;= 5) { ... }   // now prints 1 2 3 4 5
Rule of thumb: trace the last iteration by hand. Ask: “What is i when the loop exits? Is that right?”

Off-by-one errors are among the most common bugs even for experienced programmers. The trace-the-last-iteration technique is the universal antidote. Do this slowly: when i = 5, is i &lt; 5 true or false? False — so it stops at 4. What would make it true? i &lt;= 5. Walk it out completely.

Code Example
Combining methods (decks 08–09) with a while loop:
void printMultiples(int n, int limit) {
    IO.println("Multiples of " + n + " up to " + limit + ":");
    int current = n;
    while (current &lt;= limit) {
        IO.println(current);
        current = current + n;    // update is + n, not + 1
    }
}

void main() {
    printMultiples(3, 20);
    IO.println("---");
    printMultiples(7, 50);
}
Output (first call): 3  6  9  12  15  18

This example stitches together methods with parameters (deck 09) and a while loop. Point out that the update is current + n rather than current + 1 — the loop can skip by any amount, not just 1. Ask students to trace the first call manually with a variable table.

