The else if Chain

else if lets you check multiple mutually exclusive cases in sequence. Java evaluates from top to bottom and stops at the first condition that is true. Only one branch ever runs — and once a branch runs, all subsequent else if and else branches are skipped. The grade classifier is the canonical example: score &gt;= 90 is A, score &gt;= 80 is B, etc. This also sets up the ordering problem perfectly, which gets its own slide next.

if/else handles two cases. What if there are more?
English: “If score is 90 or above → A. If 80 or above → B. If 70 or above → C. If 60 or above → D. Otherwise → F.”
if (score &gt;= 90) {
    IO.println("A");
} else if (score &gt;= 80) {
    IO.println("B");
} else if (score &gt;= 70) {
    IO.println("C");
} else if (score &gt;= 60) {
    IO.println("D");
} else {
    IO.println("F");
}
Java checks from top to bottom and stops at the first true condition.
Only one branch ever runs — never two, never zero.



