for s in scores:
    print(s)


Java’s enhanced for-loop and Python’s for x in list do the same thing: hand you each element in turn, no index needed
Need the index too? for i, s in enumerate(scores): gives you both — closest Python equivalent to a classic indexed loop


~2 min. Students will reach for for i in range(len(scores)): print(scores[i]) out of habit — show it works, then show for s in scores: is the idiomatic version and gets used almost everywhere in this course.

While Loops and Conditionals


Java
int n = 10;
while (n &gt; 0) {
    n--;
}
if (n == 0 && ready) {
    n = 1;
}

Python
n = 10
while n &gt; 0:
    n -= 1

if n == 0 and ready:
    n = 1


&&, ||, ! in Java become the words and, or, not in Python
No n-- or n++ shorthand in Python — write n -= 1 / n += 1
== still means “equals” in both languages — that one didn’t change


~2 min. Quick slide, mostly a reference table students will return to — don’t over-explain, just point out the word-vs-symbol swap.

Functions, Side by Side


Java
static double average(
        int a, int b) {
    return (a + b) / 2.0;
}

Python
def average(a, b):
    return (a + b) / 2


def replaces the return-type-plus-name pattern — Python functions don’t declare a return type
No static, no access modifiers (public/private) — Python’s simplicity here is deliberate, not something being hidden
Python allows default arguments: def average(a, b=0): lets a caller omit b entirely — Java requires overloading a whole separate method for that


~3 min. Default arguments are new territory for a Java-only background — worth a concrete second example (greet(name, greeting="Hello"), called both with and without greeting).

Worked Example: Scalar Multiply
@cuda.jit
def multiply_scalar(arr, scalar):
    i = cuda.grid(1)
    if i &lt; arr.shape[0]:
        arr[i] = arr[i] * scalar

One thread per array element — for N = 500,000 elements, the GPU launches 500,000 threads
No thread needs any other thread’s result — this is the “embarrassingly parallel” shape from Module 1
This is Lab 04 Exercise 1’s pattern; you’ll write the launch configuration yourself in lab


~2 min. Deliberately simple — the goal here is the launch pattern sinking in, not the arithmetic.

Worked Example: Add Two Arrays
@cuda.jit
def add_arrays(a, b, out):
    i = cuda.grid(1)
    if i &lt; out.shape[0]:
        out[i] = a[i] + b[i]

Three arrays now live on the device: two inputs, one output
Still no dependencies between elements — output[i] only ever needs input[i], never any other index
Ask yourself: if N doubles, what (if anything) changes in the launch configuration? (Answer: BLOCKS recomputes via the same ceiling-division formula — THREADS usually stays fixed)


~2 min. Lab 04 Exercise 2’s pattern. The “what changes when N doubles” question is worth pausing on — it’s testing whether students actually understand ceiling division rather than having memorized a specific number.

Preview: The Capstone’s Kernel
@cuda.jit
def similarity_naive(Q, D, sim):
    row, col = cuda.grid(2)          # row = query index, col = database index
    if row &lt; Q.shape[0] and col &lt; D.shape[0]:
        acc = 0.0
        for k in range(Q.shape[1]):  # over the embedding dimension (64)
            acc += Q[row, k] * D[col, k]
        sim[row, col] = acc

This is the heart of the semester-long capstone, in outline form — one thread per (query, database-item) pair
At capstone scale (1,000 queries × 100,000 database items) that’s 100 million threads, each running this exact loop independently
You’ll fill in a version of this kernel’s gaps at CK-13; Module 4 will later replace the inner loop with tiled shared-memory access for a major speedup


~2 min. This is purely a preview — students are not expected to write this today. The point is recognition: “oh, that’s just a 2D kernel with an extra loop inside,” which is exactly true.

Thread Divergence: When Threads Disagree
@cuda.jit
def classify(arr, out):
    i = cuda.grid(1)
    if arr[i] &gt; 0:
        out[i] = arr[i] * 2.0      # "positive" path
    else:
        out[i] = arr[i] * -1.0     # "negative" path

Every thread in a warp executes the same instruction together — but what happens when an if sends different threads down different paths?
Within one warp, some threads may satisfy arr[i] &gt; 0 and others may not — the warp cannot run two different instructions at once
This situation is called thread divergence, and it has a real, measurable cost


~2 min. Set this up as a genuine puzzle before revealing the answer — ask “if the swim team’s coach calls two different moves to different swimmers at once, what actually happens?”

Coalesced Access
@cuda.jit
def coalesced_kernel(arr, out):
    i = cuda.grid(1)
    if i &lt; arr.shape[0]:
        out[i] = arr[i] * 2.0   # thread i reads element i

Thread 0 reads element 0, thread 1 reads element 1, …, thread 31 reads element 31 — 32 consecutive threads, 32 consecutive addresses
This falls into a single memory transaction (or a small, fixed number) covering one contiguous cache line
Every element-wise kernel you’ve written since Module 2 (arr[i] = arr[i] * scalar, out[i] = a[i] + b[i]) already has this property — you’ve been writing coalesced kernels without necessarily naming the pattern


~2 min. Reassuring framing: this isn’t a new skill to learn from scratch, it’s a name for something students have already been doing correctly since Lab 4. What’s new is recognizing when a kernel accidentally breaks this pattern (next slide).

2D Access Patterns
# Row access: consecutive in memory — coalesced ✓
@cuda.jit
def row_kernel(mat, out):
    row, col = cuda.grid(2)
    if row &lt; mat.shape[0] and col &lt; mat.shape[1]:
        out[row, col] = mat[row, col] * 2.0

# Column access: strided in memory — not coalesced ✗
@cuda.jit
def col_kernel(mat, out):
    row, col = cuda.grid(2)
    if row &lt; mat.shape[0] and col &lt; mat.shape[1]:
        out[col, row] = mat[row, col]   # transpose — watch the indices

NumPy (and Numba) arrays are row-major by default: element [r, c] sits at memory offset r * n_cols + c
Consecutive threads with the same row, increasing column → consecutive memory offsets → coalesced (the row_kernel above)
Consecutive threads with the same column, increasing row → offsets that jump by n_cols each time → strided, not coalesced (the transpose pattern in col_kernel)
Lab 8’s Exercise 3 has you measure exactly this row-vs-column contrast on a real matrix


~3 min. The row-major offset formula (r * n_cols + c) is worth writing out explicitly — it’s the concrete arithmetic reason “same row, varying column” is coalesced and “same column, varying row” isn’t, rather than something to take on faith.

A Worked Example: Block-Level Maximum
@cuda.jit
def block_max_kernel(arr, out):
    s = cuda.shared.array(shape=256, dtype=numba.float32)
    i  = cuda.grid(1)
    ti = cuda.threadIdx.x

    if i &lt; arr.shape[0]:
        s[ti] = arr[i]
    else:
        s[ti] = -1e9            # identity element for max
    cuda.syncthreads()

    # Reduction to find block maximum — stride halving
    stride = 128
    while stride &gt; 0:
        if ti &lt; stride:
            if s[ti + stride] &gt; s[ti]:
                s[ti] = s[ti + stride]
        cuda.syncthreads()
        stride //= 2

    if ti == 0:
        out[cuda.blockIdx.x] = s[0]

~2 min. FIXED this session: this slide used to be titled “Lab 08: Block Statistics” and claimed to be “Lab 08 Exercise 1 in conceptual form,” and a separate slide’s notes (the “Rules of Shared Memory” slide above) claimed “Lab 08 Exercise 3” demonstrated a forgotten-syncthreads bug — both stale. Lab 8 is Memory Coalescing (Session · Oct 21, top of this file), with no shared-memory content at all; both are leftovers from an older, archived version of Lab 8 (see labs/archive/lab08-shared-memory-superseded.md). This kernel is kept purely as a worked illustration of the shared-memory + syncthreads pattern — the real, graded double-syncthreads pattern is Lab 9’s tiled matmul (Oct 28, below); the graded version of this specific stride-halving reduction is taught properly in Module 5 (Nov 2, CK-10).

Tiled Kernel: Loop Over Tiles
    n_tiles = (A.shape[1] + TILE - 1) // TILE

    for t in range(n_tiles):
        # Load tile from global → shared
        if row &lt; A.shape[0] and t * TILE + ty &lt; A.shape[1]:
            tile_A[tx, ty] = A[row, t * TILE + ty]
        else:
            tile_A[tx, ty] = 0.0

        if t * TILE + tx &lt; B.shape[0] and col &lt; B.shape[1]:
            tile_B[tx, ty] = B[t * TILE + tx, col]
        else:
            tile_B[tx, ty] = 0.0

        cuda.syncthreads()              # wait for all loads

        for k in range(TILE):
            acc += tile_A[tx, k] * tile_B[k, ty]

        cuda.syncthreads()              # wait before overwriting shared

    if row &lt; C.shape[0] and col &lt; C.shape[1]:
        C[row, col] = acc

The two syncthreads() calls are the part almost everyone gets wrong the first time — the next slide explains exactly why both are required


~4 min. Walk through the loop body in order: bounds-checked load into tile_A/tile_B (zero-padding out-of-range entries so the accumulation stays correct even when the matrix size isn’t a multiple of TILE), first sync, the inner k loop accumulating from shared memory only, second sync. CK-14 (capstone) has two gaps in exactly this shape.

The Console I/O You Already Know
Before diving into files, recall how you handled console I/O in CS1:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
